    "styleType": "classic",
    "id": "note-1792260187843",
    "title": "Investigations",
    "content": "Does graph structure influence QAOA performance?\nInvestigate whether QAOA behaves differently on graphs with different structures.\nFor example:\n- Sparse versus dense graphs.\n- Random graphs versus regular graphs.\n- Smaller versus larger instances.\nYour goal would be to understand whether particular graph characteristics are associated with better or worse performance.\nCan we improve how QAOA parameters are initialized?\nQAOA uses parameters that a classical optimizer adjusts to find good solutions.\nYou could investigate whether a particular initialization strategy helps QAOA find good solutions more reliably or with fewer optimization steps.\nYour contribution might be a new strategy, but only if the literature review establishes that it is meaningfully different from existing methods and your experiments support its value.\nCan graph characteristics help us choose an optimization strategy?\nThis is the direction I would initially investigate.\nInstead of asking only which algorithm performs better, ask:\nCan simple graph characteristics help predict whether QAOA or a classical heuristic will perform better on a particular Max-Cut instance?\nYou could investigate features such as graph density, degree distribution, and connectivity.\nYou would then test whether those features are associated with differences in solution quality or computational cost.\nThis could potentially lead to a useful decision rule for selecting an algorithm.\nHowever, it is a hypothesis, not a confirmed research gap. Related work on graph-aware QAOA and performance prediction exists, so we must investigate that work before committing to this direction.",
